<h2 id="how">How to use this table</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Find your level.</strong> It is printed on your payslip as "Level" in the pay matrix.</li>
<li><strong>Find your own cell.</strong> If you have served for several years, your basic pay is higher than the entry cell. Multiply your own basic by the factor. The <a href="/8th-pay-commission-salary-calculator/">salary calculator</a> does it for you.</li>
<li><strong>Remember DA.</strong> Dearness Allowance at 63% already adds to your pay today. A new matrix usually absorbs DA, so the real gain is smaller than the factor suggests. See the <a href="/fitment-factor-8th-pay-commission/">fitment factor guide</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="gap">Why the minimum and maximum matter</h2>
<p>Level 1 is the lowest entry pay (Rs 18,000) and Level 18 the highest (Rs 2,50,000, the Cabinet Secretary). Staff bodies have asked for a minimum of Rs 69,000, which is Level 1 at a factor of about 3.83. The commission has made no decision. Read the <a href="/8th-pay-commission-demands-interim-relief/">demands</a> and the <a href="/minimum-basic-pay-8th-pay-commission/">minimum basic pay</a> guide.</p>

